1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
You've heard about the War on Drugs for years, right?

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:06,160
It's always been framed as a campaign to keep us safe.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:09,160
But what if that was never the real story?

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:13,360
What if the whole thing was actually built on a political calculation?

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,200
Well, we're gonna dive deep into that.

6
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,320
And it all starts with one absolutely jaw-dropping quote.

7
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,760
Yeah, you heard that right.

8
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:25,240
That is a direct quote, an on-the-record admission from a key figure,

9
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:29,520
someone who was right there in the inner circle when the Modern War on Drugs was launched.

10
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:33,360
A statement like that, it just reframes everything, doesn't it?

11
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,960
So the big question is, who on earth would say something like that?

12
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:38,280
And why?

13
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:43,880
Okay, so to really get how big of a deal this confession is, you have to know who said it.

14
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:48,600
And let me tell you, this wasn't some low-level staffer or an intern, nope.

15
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,120
This came straight from the top.

16
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,960
The man's name was John Ehrlichman,

17
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:58,280
and he was President Richard Nixon's chief of domestic policy.

18
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:00,080
I mean, think about that role for a second.

19
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,240
This guy was one of the most powerful people in the White House.

20
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:06,400
He was an architect of their entire domestic agenda.

21
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,920
That means he had a front-row seat to the real conversations,

22
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:14,840
the ones that happened behind closed doors, far away from any cameras or reporters.

23
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:16,960
So his words aren't just speculation,

24
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:20,920
they're an eyewitness account from inside the room where it all happened.

25
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:25,640
So here it is, the full strategy straight from Ehrlichman himself.

26
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:28,200
He said the Nixon campaign had two enemies.

27
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:31,040
The anti-war left and black people.

28
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:34,200
Now, obviously, you can't just make it illegal to protest a war,

29
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:36,600
and you certainly can't make it illegal to be black.

30
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,040
So what did they do?

31
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:39,680
They found a workaround.

32
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:43,080
They worked to get the public to associate hippies with marijuana

33
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,480
and black communities with heroin.

34
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:49,360
Then you pass laws that heavily criminalize both of those drugs.

35
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:53,600
And just like that, you have a perfectly legal tool to disrupt those communities.

36
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:55,720
You can arrest their leaders, raid their homes,

37
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:00,120
and in his words, vilify them night after night on the evening news.

38
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:04,320
The key takeaway here is just, wow, it was never really about the drugs.

39
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,120
The drugs were the weapon.

40
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:10,960
What Ehrlichman described is a very specific and pretty cynical tactic,

41
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:15,520
but it's actually just one example of a much broader political playbook.

42
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,160
It's a playbook based on shifting your language to achieve the same goals

43
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:23,320
when the old ways of speaking become, well, socially unacceptable.

44
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:24,920
And get a load of this.

45
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:28,080
This is another one of those incredibly candid quotes,

46
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:31,360
this time from the famous Republican strategist, Lee Atwater.

47
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:35,080
He's explaining the mechanics of what became known as the Southern Strategy.

48
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:37,320
And what's wild is that he's not even defending it.

49
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:40,800
He's dissecting it like a mechanic taking apart an engine.

50
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,120
He's saying that as society changed and overt racism became political poison,

51
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:46,720
the messaging had to evolve.

52
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:48,640
It had to become more abstract.

53
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:51,960
And this slide just illustrates that evolution perfectly.

54
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,480
Atwater breaks it down into clear steps.

55
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,040
You start with the explicit, ugly stuff.

56
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:59,680
When that stops working for you politically, you pivot.

57
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,480
You move to coded policy terms like states' rights or forced busing,

58
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,000
things that were widely understood at the time to be about race

59
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
without explicitly saying so.

60
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,560
And then it gets even more abstract.

61
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:14,080
You start talking in purely economic terms, like cutting taxes,

62
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:18,520
which, as Atwater himself noted, could still have a racially disparate impact.

63
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:20,920
The language gets more and more indirect,

64
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:24,280
but the intended effect can stay exactly the same.

65
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:26,400
There's a political term for this tactic.

66
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:27,880
It's called a dog whistle.

67
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:29,760
And the analogy is perfect, right?

68
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:32,960
A real dog whistle makes a sound only dogs can hear.

69
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:36,240
Well, a political dog whistle is language designed to sound harmless

70
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:39,320
to the general population, but to send a very different,

71
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:42,880
very specific message to a targeted subgroup of voters.

72
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:46,120
It's the core mechanism behind that abstract attack.

73
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:47,400
Now, you might be thinking,

74
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,640
okay, this is some pretty intense stuff from the 1960s.

75
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:53,840
You know, a product of that specific turbulent time.

76
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:57,960
But the truth is, this playbook of linking drugs to minority groups

77
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,840
to justify a policy, yeah, that was an old trick

78
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,760
even before Nixon got to the White House.

79
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:07,040
Let's wind the clock all the way back to the 1930s.

80
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:11,040
The very first person in charge of the nation's Federal Drug Enforcement Agency

81
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:13,760
was a man named Harry J. Anslinger.

82
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:17,760
And the rhetoric he used to push for the criminalization of marijuana,

83
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:21,560
well, it's set a precedent that would echo for decades to come.

84
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,320
I mean, just listen to Anslinger's own words here.

85
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:27,440
He's not talking about public health data or medical research.

86
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,040
He is explicitly tying marijuana to race,

87
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,800
Negroes, Hispanics, Filipinos, and to culture

88
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,880
that he clearly disapproved of, calling jazz music satanic.

89
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:38,400
This wasn't a subtle dog whistle.

90
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:39,880
This was a bullhorn.

91
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:41,840
It shows that from the very, very beginning,

92
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,840
American drug policy was all tangled up

93
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:46,480
with racial and cultural anxieties.

94
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,640
So we've seen the political strategy

95
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:50,840
and we've seen its historical roots.

96
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:54,600
But this was never just an abstract game of words and policies.

97
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,560
This war had real devastating consequences,

98
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:59,720
measured in millions of human lives.

99
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:01,720
Let's just look at the numbers.

100
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:05,760
This timeline is, honestly, it's staggering.

101
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:08,800
When Nixon declared his war on drugs in 1971,

102
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:12,560
the entire U.S. prison population was around 300,000.

103
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:14,240
Then Reagan expanded the war.

104
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:17,360
Then Clinton signed the big tough-on-crime bill in 94.

105
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,160
And look at where we are today.

106
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:21,960
2.3 million people behind bars.

107
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:25,440
The policies put in place had a direct, dramatic,

108
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:29,680
and truly explosive effect on incarceration in this country.

109
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:32,760
And let's just focus on that number for a second.

110
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:36,360
Since 1980, the number of people in prison or jail,

111
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:38,160
specifically for a drug offense,

112
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:41,680
has increased by 1,100%.

113
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:44,520
Not 100, 1100%.

114
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:45,920
That's not a gradual shift.

115
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:50,200
That is a seismic explosion in one single category of crime.

116
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:53,280
So who did this explosion affect the most?

117
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:55,680
This chart tells a pretty clear story.

118
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:59,360
Black Americans make up about 13% of the U.S. population,

119
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:02,520
but they account for 38% of all drug arrests

120
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:06,000
and an absolutely unbelievable 59% of those

121
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,320
in state prison for drug offenses.

122
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:09,280
And here's the thing.

123
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,560
Research consistently shows that white people

124
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:14,680
and black people use drugs at very similar rates.

125
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:16,760
So the disparity isn't about who's using.

126
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:19,120
It's about who's being punished.

127
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:22,160
And nothing illustrates this disparity more clearly

128
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:25,640
than the infamous laws around crack versus powder cocaine.

129
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:27,600
Pharmacologically, they're the same drug.

130
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,840
But crack, which was associated with poor black communities,

131
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:33,280
got you a mandatory five-year prison sentence

132
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:35,120
for just five grams.

133
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:38,360
Powder cocaine, associated with more affluent white users,

134
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:39,920
required 500 grams.

135
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:41,600
That's 100 times the amount

136
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:43,680
to trigger that exact same sentence.

137
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:48,120
The result, 93% of those convicted for crack offenses were black.

138
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:50,640
Just let that sink in for a moment.

139
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:55,320
This comparison provides some really crucial global context.

140
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:58,040
It's a shocking statistic that forces us to grapple

141
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:00,880
with the sheer scale and the racial disparity

142
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:03,680
of mass incarceration in the United States.

143
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:06,120
So how did this all happen in an era

144
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:09,120
that claimed to be moving past over discrimination?

145
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:11,720
Well, it all comes back to where we started.

146
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:14,480
The power of language and the incredible success

147
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:16,960
of that abstract attack.

148
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:19,880
And this is really the final piece of the puzzle.

149
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:22,240
The whole political conversation shifted.

150
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:23,960
Instead of arguing about segregation,

151
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:25,840
which was no longer acceptable,

152
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:28,440
politicians started arguing about crime.

153
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,200
This allowed racially biased outcomes to continue,

154
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,360
not through explicitly racist laws,

155
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:36,840
but through a new system of so-called colorblind law enforcement

156
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:39,600
that was, in practice, anything but.

157
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:42,200
So what's the big takeaway from all this?

158
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:44,040
I think it's about the importance of developing

159
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:46,560
what some people call critical consciousness.

160
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,240
It's just the process of becoming aware

161
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:51,920
of how these huge social and political systems actually work,

162
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,400
seeing the connections between language, policy,

163
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:55,920
and their real-world impact.

164
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,120
Because understanding why something happened

165
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:01,720
is always the first step toward figuring out how to change it.

166
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:03,880
And that brings us to our final thought.

167
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,200
This playbook, identifying a political enemy,

168
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:08,000
linking them to a perceived threat,

169
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,960
and using abstract, coded language

170
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:11,880
to mobilize public opinion against them,

171
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:13,880
is not a relic of history.

172
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:15,840
It didn't just disappear.

173
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:18,040
So the crucial question we're left with is this.

174
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:19,360
Now that you know the playbook,

175
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:21,200
now that you can recognize the pattern,

176
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:22,640
where else do you see it playing out

177
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:24,440
in the world around you right now?

